<h2>The settlement and its breach</h2>
<p>The parties had settled their disputes by a mutual settlement dated 13 November 2025, recorded by the Supreme Court on 12 December 2025 in a pending transfer petition. Under it, both children were to remain in the mother's custody; the father received visitation twice a year, four days at a time, at his parents' home in Dholpur, Rajasthan, and agreed to pay Rs 8,000 per month towards the children's education.</p>
<p>The mother's contempt petition alleged that on 22 July 2026 the father took the son from school and kept him in Rajasthan. Notice issued on 28 September 2026. The father's counter-affidavit did not deny the act; it justified it by the child's stated wish to stay with him and claimed the boy was now settled at Dholpur.</p>

<h2>The in-chambers interaction</h2>
<p>On 3 November 2026 the Bench interacted with the child in chambers. The order records a troubling impression: the child appeared "severely tutored" against his mother and sister, and insisted on staying with the father. The Court also recorded a prima facie observation — without any finding — that the father appeared to display signs of substance abuse.</p>

<h2>Contempt found, enforcement calibrated</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Finding:</strong> the father "is in clear contempt" of the order of 12 December 2025.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Restraint:</strong> no immediate return of the child, to avoid unnecessary complications and lasting harm to the child's psychology.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Remedy:</strong> the Family Court, Dholpur, is to engage a child counsellor to work on restoring the child's trust in his mother and sister; the father must bring the child to every session, must not interfere, and bears the counsellor's cost.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Review:</strong> the child stays at Dholpur for now; the counsellor's report comes in a sealed cover on 22 January 2027, with early listing possible if the child is ready to return sooner.</div>
</div>
<p>No punishment was imposed at this stage — the order is interim, and the contempt jurisdiction is being used as a lever to repair the situation rather than simply to punish the default.</p>

<h2>Why this order matters in custody practice</h2>
<p>Three features deserve attention. First, the Court refused to let the child's expressed preference launder the breach: the contempt finding came notwithstanding the child's insistence on staying with the father, precisely because tutoring was suspected. Second, the Court decoupled liability from remedy — a parent can be in clear contempt and yet immediate restitution may be withheld where the child's welfare demands a staged return. Third, the choice of a court-appointed counsellor, reporting in sealed cover on a fixed date, gives the arrangement structure and accountability that informal assurances never provide.</p>
